xmodel/sol-msolve-fix.log:14:You are GPT 5.6 Sol, open-source contributor for the JC2 math campaign. TASK: fix a crash bug in msolve (the open-source Groebner basis library, github.com/algebraic-solving/msolve) that we triggered with a large legitimate polynomial system, and contribute the fix upstream as a PR. This is ordinary OSS maintenance: our input is a valid 86-row/172-var polynomial system over F_105337 and msolve 0.10.1 exits with a memory fault instead of computing -- likely an integer-overflow or allocation-size bug on very large rows (some have 2.4M terms). MATERIALS on box01 (ubuntu@54.175.21.169, key ~/.ssh/claude-cli.pem): the failing input ~/jc72108/cases43/d43fam_p105337_a00pp_verdict.ms (+ p105673 sibling); the bracketing pair d43split_p105337_upto38.ms (works) vs upto40.ms (crashes); draft notes ops/msolve-issue-draft.md. STEPS: (1) build msolve from source on box01 with debug info (./autogen.sh && ./configure CFLAGS="-g -O1" && make -j); (2) minimize the failing input (delta between upto38/upto40, then try trimming rows/terms) and capture a stack trace of the fault; (3) locate the defect in the source (check size arithmetic in F4 symbolic preprocessing / exponent packing / hash table growth for 32-bit overflow at multi-million-term scale) and write the smallest correct fix; (4) rebuild, confirm the minimized input and the original file now run (or proceed past the old fault), run the project test suite; (5) using local gh (authenticated): fork, branch, commit, push, open a PR with a MINIMAL body -- 2-3 sentences: what was wrong, the fix, and the crash evidence (trace excerpt + minimized input); no campaign context. (6) Bank PR URL + summary in ops/msolve-issue-draft.md + dated notes.md entry. If the defect is too invasive to fix safely, open an issue with reproducer + trace instead and bank that.
