./xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:6532:1. **The “fully adjudicated book” is not exhaustive.** [BOOK-ENUM.md](/Users/dc/code/math/jc72108/BOOK-ENUM.md:35) explicitly leaves every entry multiset containing \(b_i\ge2\)—hence \(M_{\rm entry}\ge2\)—uncalculated; mixed \(\mu\ge2\) merges are also quarantined. Yet [BOOK-BASH-R2.md](/Users/dc/code/math/jc72108/BOOK-BASH-R2.md:222) declares \(td=7,11,13\) fully excluded. Those prime panels are entirely “off-axis,” not empty. A counterexample there never enters the 421-cell enumeration. The \(td=6\) all-\(M=1\) book is unaffected, but the advertised \(td\le14\) completeness is false.
./xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:6534:2. **The mass identity \(td=\sum_{F\in T_{a,\mathrm{pole}}}\Lambda(F)\) is unproved at the strength consumed.** [SIGRAY-AUDIT.md](/Users/dc/code/math/jc72108/SIGRAY-AUDIT.md:74) reconstructs Proposition 5.8 only for generic \(a\), not “every \(a\).” This identity supplies every entry partition, \(m\le td/3\), the \(3+3\) reduction at \(td=6\), the prime theorem, and the book’s layer E. A generic-fiber reformulation may repair it, but that argument is presently missing.
./xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:6536:3. **“The full JC2 program is finite at every level” confuses pointwise finiteness with termination.** Finiteness for each fixed \((m,td)\) gives infinitely many books; only \(td\le14\) was enumerated and the degree farm stops at 150. Moreover, killing residue-A would still leave four single-pole \(td=6\) classes, while the composite single-pole residual grows to 212 classes at \(td=16\). There is no uniform \(td\)-bound, induction, or stabilization theorem. Thus “one template away from JC2” is not justified.
